import RequestProject.Wikidata.Diagnostics

/-!
# Property characteristics beyond the class fragment

The last gap of `RELATED_WORK.md` §2.10 is the OWL-level vocabulary outside the
class/instance/part-of fragment.  The part of it that Wikidata really carries is
*property characteristics*: a property item can be declared transitive
(`Q18647515`), symmetric (`Q18647518`), or the inverse of another property
(`P1696`), and consumers routinely reason with those declarations.

A profile (`Owl.Profile`) records which properties have which characteristic, and
`Owl.Entails` is the resulting entailment relation over the truthy triples of a
knowledge base.  A base is *closed* when it already contains everything the
profile entails from it, and closure is decided by an exact checker
(`Owl.closedOk_iff`); on a closed base entailment is exactly membership
(`Owl.mem_of_entails_of_closed`), which is the sense in which the checker is
complete.

The boundary results are the point:

* **Declaring a property transitive is not conservative**
  (`transitivity_is_not_conservative`): the declaration alone adds facts the base
  never asserted, so it is an edit to the theory and not a reading of it — the
  same review-gating argument the repair layer makes for class merges.
* **An inverse declaration makes the converse derived, not recorded**
  (`converse_is_derived_not_recorded`): the base records one direction, the
  profile yields the other, and no consumer can tell from the derived triple
  which of the two was asserted.
* **Class equivalence must not be encoded as a subclass cycle**
  (`equivalence_by_cycle_is_invalid`): a pair of mutual `P279` statements makes
  the base invalid by this project's own diagnostics, so `owl:equivalentClass`
  needs a predicate of its own — which is what the alignment layer provides.
-/

